---
/*
  A parallax panorama: artwork sliced into layers that drift past each other
  as the section crosses the viewport.

  PROTOTYPE. Nothing in Studio makes one of these yet - it is placed by hand on
  /style-guide with the placeholder slices in public/parallax-demo/ so the FEEL
  can be judged on a real phone before a CMS block is built around it. The
  shape of the data is the thing still in question: how many layers, whether
  depth is authored per layer or derived from order, vertical or horizontal
  drift. Those are much easier to answer after scrolling one.

  HOW IT MOVES, AND WHY THERE IS NO JAVASCRIPT

  animation-timeline: view() ties an animation's progress to how far this
  element has travelled through the viewport. The browser runs it off the main
  thread, so it does not stutter the way a scroll listener recalculating
  transforms does - and this site already uses the same mechanism for the
  fade-and-rise on cards and article prose, so it is a pattern in use here
  rather than a new dependency.

  Each layer drifts by its own --depth. 0 pins a layer to the page; larger
  numbers move further, so a foreground slice outruns a background one and the
  gap between them reads as distance.

  THE STILL VERSION IS THE REAL DESIGN

  Everything below is inside @supports, and browser support is uneven - Chrome
  and Edge have had it since 115, Safari only recently, Firefox later still.
  Where it is missing the layers simply stack and sit, which has to look
  deliberate rather than broken. That is a constraint on how the artwork gets
  sliced, not something CSS can fix: compose the still image first, and treat
  the motion as something the capable browsers get on top.

  Same for prefers-reduced-motion, which this honours - parallax is one of the
  effects that reliably makes people motion-sick, so the static composition is
  what a reader who asked for less movement gets.

  WEIGHT. Layers mean transparency, which means PNG, which does not compress
  the way the photography on this site does. These placeholders are SVG and
  weigh 16 KB for all four; real sliced artwork will not. When this becomes a
  Studio block, the compression toggle wants to be OFF for these so the CDN
  can serve WebP with alpha - the opposite of the usual habit here.
*/

interface Layer {
  /** Path or URL of the slice. Transparent PNG, or anything with an alpha channel. */
  src: string;
  /**
   * How far this layer drifts, as a percentage of its own height.
   * 0 pins it. Around 4 reads as far away, around 20 as close.
   */
  depth: number;
  /** Empty for a decorative slice - the panorama as a whole carries the alt. */
  alt?: string;
}

interface Props {
  layers: Layer[];
  /** What the whole composition depicts, for screen readers. */
  label: string;
  /** Aspect ratio of the window the layers are seen through. */
  ratio?: string;
  /*
    And on a phone, where a 16:9 window is a 201px strip - too thin to show
    artwork, and too short to give the drift anywhere to go, since the travel
    is a percentage of this box's height. Portrait by default.
  */
  ratioMobile?: string;
  caption?: string;
}

const { layers, label, ratio = '16 / 9', ratioMobile = '4 / 5', caption } = Astro.props;
---

{/*
  role="img" goes on the WINDOW, not the figure.

  A composite of slices is one picture, and role="img" plus a label is how you
  say that - the individual layers then carry alt="" correctly, because none of
  them is a picture on its own. But role="img" also makes everything inside it
  presentational, so putting it on the <figure> would have taken the
  <figcaption> down with it: the caption would be on the page and unreadable to
  a screen reader. On the window, the figure keeps its normal structure and the
  caption is still announced with it.
*/}
<figure
  class="panorama"
  style={`--panorama-ratio: ${ratio}; --panorama-ratio-mobile: ${ratioMobile}`}
>
  <div class="panorama__window" role="img" aria-label={label}>
    {layers.map((layer, i) => (
      <img
        class="panorama__layer"
        src={layer.src}
        alt=""
        /*
          Eager on the first layer, lazy after. A panorama that scrolls into
          view with its background still blank reads as broken, and the
          backdrop is the one layer that covers the whole window.
        */
        loading={i === 0 ? 'eager' : 'lazy'}
        decoding="async"
        style={`--depth: ${layer.depth}`}
      />
    ))}
  </div>
  {caption && <figcaption class="panorama__caption">{caption}</figcaption>}
</figure>
